# SqlGenerator interface

The direct `SqlGenerator` interface generates SQL from a [generation plan](plan.md). Use it when you need a reusable plan, a grammar rule not exposed by a provider method, or constraints on productions and token spellings. It uses Faker for randomness without requiring a SQL provider to be registered.

## Common

Each dialect exposes a final `SqlGenerator` class with the same calling convention:

| Operation | Parameters and result |
|-----------|-----------------------|
| Constructor | The dialect's loaded grammar, a `Faker\Generator`, and `?string $version = null` |
| `generate(GenerationPlan $plan): string` | Generate one result from the supplied plan |

There is no shared `SqlGenerator` PHP interface or base class. The common contract described here is implemented by each dialect class.

### Construction

Load the grammar and pass the same version tag to the generator. The generator does not infer a version from the loaded grammar object. An explicit version enables terminal-coverage validation and the lexical token-sequence check described in the [algorithm](algorithm.md).

| Dialect | Generator class | Grammar loader | Default version |
|---------|-----------------|----------------|-----------------|
| MySQL | `SqlFaker\MySql\SqlGenerator` | `SqlFaker\MySql\Grammar\Grammar::load()` | `mysql-8.4.7` |
| PostgreSQL | `SqlFaker\PostgreSql\SqlGenerator` | `SqlFaker\PostgreSql\Grammar\PgGrammar::load()` | `pg-17.2` |
| SQLite | `SqlFaker\Sqlite\SqlGenerator` | `SqlFaker\Sqlite\Grammar\SqliteGrammar::load()` | `sqlite-3.47.2` |

The MySQL loader returns `SqlFaker\MySql\Grammar\Grammar`. The PostgreSQL and SQLite loaders return `SqlFaker\Grammar\Grammar`. Each loader also exposes `resolveVersion(?string $version = null): string`, which resolves the default or validates a supplied tag.

Omitting the generator's version selects the default lexical model and allows synthetic terminals. In that mode, it skips terminal-coverage validation and does not compare the realized token sequence with the expected sequence. For bundled grammars, use an explicit resolved version as in the examples below.

### Plans and results

`generate()` accepts a `SqlFaker\Grammar\GenerationPlan`, not a statement string or `StatementType` enum. Use `GenerationPlan::fromRule(StatementType::Select->value)` to turn an enum value into a plan. Add `withMaxDepth(6)` on the plan to control complexity; `generate()` has no separate depth argument.

A successful call returns SQL text. Depending on the start rule, this can be a statement, multiple statements, a fragment, or an empty string. `requiringNonEmpty()` requests a non-empty result and raises an exception if generation cannot satisfy it. It does not turn a fragment into a complete statement.

Plans can be reused. Every call generates a fresh result using the current Faker random state; it does not replay the previous result or remember names as a schema. Seed Faker before a sequence of calls to reproduce that sequence under the same versions and settings.

### Errors

| Exception | Typical cause |
|-----------|---------------|
| `RuntimeException` | Unsupported version or unavailable/invalid grammar or lexical resources |
| `InvalidArgumentException` | Invalid plan arguments or an unknown lexical generation target |
| `LogicException` | A grammar rule has no viable or matching alternative, non-empty output cannot be produced, or the 5,000-expansion limit is exceeded |
| `SqlFaker\Grammar\LexicalException` | An unsupported token or requested spelling, or lexical realization still failing after the available attempts |

Grammar-based generation can make up to 32 lexical attempts. It does not retry every kind of error; for example, contradictory production constraints and expansion-limit failures are reported immediately. Fix the plan or select a compatible rule/version when an error is structural. Smaller `maxDepth` values can help control large random derivations. See [limitations](algorithm.md#limitations) for the scope of successful output.

## MySQL

```php
use Faker\Factory;
use SqlFaker\Grammar\GenerationPlan;
use SqlFaker\MySql\Grammar\Grammar;
use SqlFaker\MySql\SqlGenerator;
use SqlFaker\MySql\StatementType;

$faker = Factory::create();
$faker->seed(12345);
$version = Grammar::resolveVersion('mysql-8.4.7');
$generator = new SqlGenerator(Grammar::load($version), $faker, $version);
$plan = GenerationPlan::fromRule(StatementType::Select->value)
    ->requiringNonEmpty()
    ->withMaxDepth(6);

$sql = $generator->generate($plan);
```

`GenerationPlan::all()` chooses `simple_statement_or_begin` if present, then `statement`, then the grammar's start symbol. The generator maps common modern start-rule names to older names when needed, including `select_stmt` to `select` and `create_table_stmt` to `create`. Those older rules can cover a broader statement family. This fallback does not translate every rule name or every production pattern in a custom plan.

Use `SqlFaker\MySql\GenerationPlans` for the [MySQL presets](plan.md#mysql), including `withoutEmptyRows()` and multi-table mutations. A preset must be compatible with the selected grammar version.

## PostgreSQL

```php
use Faker\Factory;
use SqlFaker\Grammar\GenerationPlan;
use SqlFaker\PostgreSql\Grammar\PgGrammar;
use SqlFaker\PostgreSql\SqlGenerator;
use SqlFaker\PostgreSql\StatementType;

$faker = Factory::create();
$faker->seed(12345);
$version = PgGrammar::resolveVersion('pg-17.2');
$generator = new SqlGenerator(PgGrammar::load($version), $faker, $version);
$plan = GenerationPlan::fromRule(StatementType::Select->value)
    ->requiringNonEmpty()
    ->withMaxDepth(6);

$sql = $generator->generate($plan);
```

`GenerationPlan::all()` starts from `stmtmulti`, which can produce empty output or multiple statements. Use `GenerationPlan::fromRule('stmt')->requiringNonEmpty()` for a general non-empty statement, or a specific enum value for a statement family. `StatementType::DropTable` maps to `DropStmt`, a broader DROP rule.

This differs from the Faker provider's parameterless `sql()`, which first chooses a random enum case. Use `SqlFaker\PostgreSql\GenerationPlans` for the [PostgreSQL presets](plan.md#postgresql), including MERGE, COPY, and TABLESAMPLE.

## SQLite

```php
use Faker\Factory;
use SqlFaker\Grammar\GenerationPlan;
use SqlFaker\Sqlite\Grammar\SqliteGrammar;
use SqlFaker\Sqlite\SqlGenerator;
use SqlFaker\Sqlite\StatementType;

$faker = Factory::create();
$faker->seed(12345);
$version = SqliteGrammar::resolveVersion('sqlite-3.47.2');
$generator = new SqlGenerator(SqliteGrammar::load($version), $faker, $version);
$plan = GenerationPlan::fromRule(StatementType::Select->value)
    ->requiringNonEmpty()
    ->withMaxDepth(6);

$sql = $generator->generate($plan);
```

`GenerationPlan::all()` starts from `cmd`. The generator provides dedicated `insert`, `update`, `delete`, `alter_table`, and `drop_table` entry points extracted from the command grammar. Optional rules such as `where_opt`, `orderby_opt`, `limit_opt`, and `with` can produce empty strings.

`StatementType::CreateTable` maps to the `create_table` opening fragment. To request a complete CREATE TABLE statement, constrain `cmd` to the alternative containing `create_table` and `create_table_args`; see the [SQLite plan example](plan.md#sqlite).

Use `SqlFaker\Sqlite\GenerationPlans::multiDmlStatement()` to request two semicolon-terminated DML statements. Unlike the provider method, the plan factory lets you choose the two statement types explicitly. Other [SQLite presets](plan.md#sqlite) cover temporary tables, views, generated columns, cascading foreign keys, upserts, and full-text syntax.

